From: nomorecoolnicknames <nomorecoolnicknames@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:18:14 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] init: also publish a socket's literal ANDROID_SOCKET_ name

Descriptor::Publish() only sets the mangled name (non-alphanumerics become
'_'). Vendor daemons built against Nougat build ANDROID_SOCKET_<name> from
the literal socket name and call getenv, so every hyphenated socket -- on
the Meizu MX6 the MediaTek RIL side bus: rild-ims, rild-oem, rild-mal-at and
others -- is invisible to them. Publish the literal name too when it
differs.

Change-Id: I40dee832011b3216e0d9ccc45636e8719577f404

diff --git a/init/service_utils.cpp b/init/service_utils.cpp
index d19f5ee..1cf1196 100644
--- a/init/service_utils.cpp
+++ b/init/service_utils.cpp
@@ -164,6 +164,18 @@ void Descriptor::Publish() const {
 
     std::string val = std::to_string(fd);
     setenv(published_name.c_str(), val.c_str(), 1);
+
+    // Vendor daemons built against Nougat find their descriptor by the socket's
+    // literal name: they build ANDROID_SOCKET_<name> themselves and call getenv
+    // instead of android_get_control_socket(), which applies the same mangling as
+    // the loop above. Nougat's init did not mangle, so every socket whose name
+    // holds a hyphen -- on the Meizu MX6 the whole MediaTek RIL side bus,
+    // rild-ims, rild-oem, rild-mal-at and others -- is invisible to them: they log
+    // a failure to acquire the socket and never listen. Publish the literal name
+    // alongside when the two differ; nothing else reads it.
+    if (name_ != published_name) {
+        setenv(name_.c_str(), val.c_str(), 1);
+    }
 }
 
 Result<Descriptor> SocketDescriptor::Create(const std::string& global_context) const {
